<PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="MEDLINE" Owner="NLM" IndexingMethod="Automated"><PMID Version="1">42849910</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>31</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>31</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Print"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">1098-4275</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><Volume>158</Volume><Issue>5</Issue><PubDate><Year>2026</Year><Month>Nov</Month><Day>01</Day></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Pediatrics</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Pediatrics</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Therapeutic Horseback Riding for Youth With Autism and Psychiatric Comorbidities: A Randomized Trial.</ArticleTitle><ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">e2026077263</ELocationID><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1542/peds.2026-077263</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Autism spectrum disorder care is often complicated by co-occurring psychiatric symptoms. Equine-assisted services show promise for improving behavioral outcomes in this population. We aimed to examine benefits of equine-assisted services for youth with autism at higher risk for mental health interventions.</AbstractText><AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A total of 236 youth with autism and psychiatric comorbidity (aged 6-16 years.) were enrolled in a randomized controlled multisite clinical trial, stratified by nonverbal IQ, and assigned to 1 of 3 intervention arms: therapeutic horseback riding (THR), no horse contact barn activity (BA), or waiting period followed by a BA/THR hybrid. Caregivers completed outcome measures. Speech outcomes were evaluated by blinded raters. Intervention methods expand those from a previous efficacy trial.</AbstractText><AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Intent-to-treat analyses with 169 participants completing at least 1 intervention lesson revealed that compared with the waitlist control (n&#x2009;=&#x2009;28), both THR (n&#x2009;=&#x2009;74) and BA (n&#x2009;=&#x2009;67) interventions showed clinically significant reductions in co-primary outcomes of irritability (P&#x2009;&lt;&#x2009;.0001; THR effect size d&#x2009;=&#x2009;1.15; BA effect size d&#x2009;=&#x2009;1.04) and hyperactivity (THR P&#x2009;&lt;&#x2009;.0001, effect size d&#x2009;=&#x2009;1.06; BA P = .005, effect size d&#x2009;=&#x2009;0.68). The THR group showed significantly greater reductions in hyperactivity compared with the BA group (P = .03; effect size d&#x2009;=&#x2009;0.39) beginning at intervention week 4. Additional significant improvements in THR and BA groups vs the waitlist control were seen in emotional regulation and social behaviors.</AbstractText><AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Equine-assisted activities are an effective intervention to address behavior challenges in youth with autism and psychiatric comorbidity; THR may offer additive reductions in hyperactivity.</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>Copyright &#xa9; 2026 by the American Academy of Pediatrics.</CopyrightInformation></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Gabriels</LastName><ForeName>Robin L</ForeName><Initials>RL</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>University of Colorado Anschutz Medical Campus, Aurora Colorado.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Children's Hospital Colorado, Aurora, Colorado.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Peters</LastName><ForeName>Caiti</ForeName><Initials>C</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Colorado State University, Fort Collins, Colorado.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Pan</LastName><ForeName>Zhaoxing</ForeName><Initials>Z</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>University of Colorado Anschutz Medical Campus, Aurora Colorado.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Children's Hospital Colorado, Aurora, Colorado.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Merritt</LastName><ForeName>Tamara</ForeName><Initials>T</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Hearts &amp; Horses Therapeutic Riding Center, Inc, Loveland, Colorado.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Widick</LastName><ForeName>Madison</ForeName><Initials>M</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>University of Colorado Anschutz Medical Campus, Aurora Colorado.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Children's Hospital Colorado, Aurora, Colorado.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Current affiliation: University of Colorado, Colorado Springs, Colorado.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Mazefsky</LastName><ForeName>Carla</ForeName><Initials>C</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>University of Pittsburgh School of Medicine, Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Siegel</LastName><ForeName>Mathew</ForeName><Initials>M</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>MaineHealth Glickman Lauder Center of Excellence in Autism and Developmental Disorders, Portland, Maine.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>MaineHealth Maine Medical Center, Portland, Maine.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Current affiliations: Boston Children's Hospital, Boston, Massachusetts.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Tufts University School of Medicine, Boston, Massachusetts.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author></AuthorList><Language>eng</Language><PublicationTypeList><PublicationType UI="D016428">Journal Article</PublicationType><PublicationType UI="D016449">Randomized Controlled Trial</PublicationType><PublicationType UI="D016448">Multicenter Study</PublicationType></PublicationTypeList></Article><MedlineJournalInfo><Country>United States</Country><MedlineTA>